PV inverter for household supply

## Overview

This design outlines a standalone residential solar inverter responsible for converting high-voltage DC power from a Photovoltaic (PV) array into stable AC power. Unlike grid-tied systems, this standalone unit must regulate its own frequency and voltage to support household appliances directly, acting as the primary energy source for the domestic AC bus.

## System Objective

The system must convert variable high-voltage DC into a regulated, clean sinusoidal AC output suitable for standard residential use.

The system must:

* **Perform DC to AC Conversion:** Transform the ~400 V DC input into a single-phase AC signal with a peak of 325 V to achieve the target RMS.
* **Implement sPWM Control:** Utilize Sinusoidal Pulse Width Modulation to drive the switching power electronics, ensuring the fundamental frequency is accurately maintained.
* **Ensure Harmonic Suppression:** Attenuate high-frequency switching noise to provide a pure sine wave, protecting sensitive inductive and electronic household loads.

## Operating Principle

High-level explanation of the power transformation and control sequence.

1. **Pulse Generation:** A high-frequency triangular carrier wave is compared against a 50 Hz reference sine wave to generate sPWM switching signals.
2. **H-Bridge Switching:** The sPWM signals drive an H-bridge inverter (using MOSFETs or IGBTs) to chop the 400 V DC into a series of variable-width pulses.
3. **Low-Pass Filtering:** An LC (Inductor-Capacitor) filter removes the high-frequency switching components, smoothing the pulses into a clean **230 V RMS** sinusoidal waveform.

